  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n - Scattered thunderstorms today into Thursday, with lightning and \n   outflow winds being the main threats. \n\n - A cold front arrives Saturday, bringing cooling temperatures, \n   gusty westerly winds, and precipitation, focused along and \n   south of I-90. \n\nThe Northern Rockies will be under the influence of a\nsouthwesterly flow pattern aloft through Friday. This pattern is\nbeing driven by a ridge of high pressure centered over the Four \nCorners region and a trough of low pressure centered over southern\nBritish Columbia. Waves of monsoon moisture will bring modest \nmoisture and instability into the area, opening the door for \nshowers and thunderstorms each day. Warm and dry air near the \nsurface will promote gusty to strong (30-50 mph) outflow wind \ngusts. The combination of dry storms, lightning, and gusty winds \nwill elevate the potential for new fire starts. The thunderstorm \nthreat will include all of the Northern Rockies this afternoon \nand evening, with activity shifting into Lemhi County, ID and \nsouthwest Montana on Thursday. A Red Flag Warning is in effect for\nthe Palouse/Hells Canyon Region, where hot, dry, and windy \nconditions are expected this afternoon and evening. \n\nForecast models have come into stronger agreement for the \naforementioned trough to push eastward across the Pacific \nNorthwest into the Northern Rockies by late Saturday into Sunday. \nA subtle drop in temperatures arrives Friday, with temperatures \ndropping further on Saturday as a cold front moves through the \nregion. While model solutions still differ on the evolution of the\ntrough and moisture availability for precipitation, the \nprobability for wetting rains has increased from 30-40% \n(yesterday) to 70-80% this weekend for areas along and south of \nI-90. Generally speaking, probabilities for precipitation decrease\nfurther north towards the US-Canadian border. Waves of monsoon \nmoisture will focus atmospheric moisture and instability along and\nsouth of I-90, driving higher chances for showers and \nthunderstorms as the trough arrives. This moisture will limit the \npotential for critical fire weather.",
